WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.617
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.917 --> 00:00:09.315
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.615 --> 00:00:16.540
Unit two: Literary Criticism. Today you will report scholarly positions using passive nominal clauses.

4
00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.771
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:23.873
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:24.717 --> 00:00:25.985
Deconstruction.

7
00:00:26.983 --> 00:00:32.466
Deconstruction means a critical method that exposes contradictions and hidden assumptions in texts.

8
00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:36.814
Deconstruction reveals how a text undermines its own claims.

9
00:00:37.417 --> 00:00:40.011
Your turn. Say: Deconstruction.

10
00:00:42.867 --> 00:00:44.158
Deconstruction.

11
00:00:44.917 --> 00:00:46.104
Postmodernism.

12
00:00:47.117 --> 00:00:52.007
Postmodernism means a movement sceptical of grand narratives and fixed meanings.

13
00:00:52.617 --> 00:00:56.334
Postmodernism questions whether any story can be objectively true.

14
00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:59.742
Your turn. Say: Postmodernism.

15
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:03.810
Postmodernism.

16
00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:05.319
Canon.

17
00:01:06.317 --> 00:01:10.406
Canon means the body of works regarded as most important in a literature.

18
00:01:11.017 --> 00:01:14.258
Khmer literature's canon includes the Reamker and Tum Teav.

19
00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.038
Your turn. Say: Canon.

20
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:20.715
Canon.

21
00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:23.032
Intertextuality.

22
00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:28.965
Intertextuality means the way texts echo, borrow from and respond to other texts.

23
00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.780
Intertextuality links the novel to the ancient Reamker.

24
00:01:33.383 --> 00:01:36.605
Your turn. Say: Intertextuality.

25
00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:40.944
Intertextuality.

26
00:01:41.683 --> 00:01:42.824
Subtext.

27
00:01:43.817 --> 00:01:47.941
Subtext means an underlying meaning that is not stated directly.

28
00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:51.943
The subtext of the dialogue is the couple's unspoken grief.

29
00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:55.237
Your turn. Say: Subtext.

30
00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.229
Subtext.

31
00:01:59.983 --> 00:02:01.147
Discourse.

32
00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:06.913
Discourse means a system of language and ideas through which a subject is discussed.

33
00:02:07.517 --> 00:02:11.142
Colonial discourse portrayed Cambodia as a lost civilisation.

34
00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:14.658
Your turn. Say: Discourse.

35
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:18.594
Discourse.

36
00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:20.641
Interpretation.

37
00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:25.101
Interpretation means an explanation of the meaning of a text.

38
00:02:25.717 --> 00:02:29.342
Each generation offers a new interpretation of Tum Teav.

39
00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:32.974
Your turn. Say: Interpretation.

40
00:02:35.833 --> 00:02:37.160
Interpretation.

41
00:02:37.917 --> 00:02:39.162
Critical lens.

42
00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:43.891
Critical lens means a theoretical perspective used to analyse a text.

43
00:02:44.483 --> 00:02:49.258
Through a feminist critical lens, the heroine's silence becomes resistance.

44
00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:52.572
Your turn. Say: Critical lens.

45
00:02:55.433 --> 00:02:56.736
Critical lens.

46
00:02:57.483 --> 00:02:58.798
Hermeneutics.

47
00:02:59.783 --> 00:03:02.874
Hermeneutics means the theory and method of interpretation.

48
00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:07.085
Hermeneutics asks how readers' histories shape what they understand.

49
00:03:07.683 --> 00:03:10.707
Your turn. Say: Hermeneutics.

50
00:03:13.567 --> 00:03:14.730
Hermeneutics.

51
00:03:15.483 --> 00:03:16.473
Close reading.

52
00:03:17.483 --> 00:03:21.410
Close reading means careful, detailed analysis of a short passage.

53
00:03:22.017 --> 00:03:25.073
Close reading of one stanza revealed the poem's irony.

54
00:03:25.683 --> 00:03:28.150
Your turn. Say: Close reading.

55
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:32.036
Close reading.

56
00:03:32.833 --> 00:03:36.751
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:37.617 --> 00:03:43.659
Visal, my teacher says Tum Teav is simply a tragic love story. Is that the only interpretation?

58
00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:51.570
Hardly. It has been argued that the poem is also a critique of arranged marriage and abusive authority.

59
00:03:52.683 --> 00:03:55.797
But surely the author's intention decides what it means?

60
00:03:56.883 --> 00:04:05.619
That view is contested. It is widely held that meaning is shaped by readers, not fixed by authors, especially when the author is unknown.

61
00:04:06.717 --> 00:04:08.879
So any interpretation is valid?

62
00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:19.686
No. It is generally accepted that a reading must be supported by textual evidence. Different lenses illuminate a text without licensing anything at all.

63
00:04:20.833 --> 00:04:22.566
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:23.417 --> 00:04:26.682
Grammar focus: Passive nominal clauses.

65
00:04:26.982 --> 00:04:28.145
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:31.802
It is argued that meaning is shaped by interpretation.

67
00:04:32.717 --> 00:04:35.947
It has been suggested that the poem critiques arranged marriage.

68
00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:40.324
It is generally accepted that readings need textual evidence.

69
00:04:41.267 --> 00:04:52.914
Impersonal passives distance the writer from a claim: It is argued / believed / widely held that… An alternative is 'subject + passive + to-infinitive': The poem is thought to date from the 17th century.

70
00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:57.986
Be careful! Don't say: It is argued the poem that is political.

71
00:04:59.933 --> 00:05:02.711
Say: It is argued that the poem is political.

72
00:05:03.011 --> 00:05:05.080
It is argued that + clause.

73
00:05:05.867 --> 00:05:08.345
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:09.133 --> 00:05:10.738
Who Owns a Text's Meaning?.

75
00:05:11.038 --> 00:05:17.229
For much of the twentieth century, it was assumed that a literary work possessed a single meaning determined by its author.

76
00:05:18.233 --> 00:05:26.885
Structuralist and post-structuralist critics challenged this assumption, and it is now widely argued that meaning emerges from the interaction between text and reader.

77
00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:33.905
Deconstruction, for instance, exposes tensions within texts that undermine their apparently stable messages.

78
00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:42.356
Critics also question the canon itself, since it has been suggested that the selection of 'great' works reflects the values of those in power.

79
00:05:43.367 --> 00:05:51.345
Nevertheless, it is generally accepted that rigorous close reading remains the foundation of persuasive criticism, whatever critical lens is adopted.

80
00:05:52.433 --> 00:05:56.412
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:57.250 --> 00:06:00.329
It blank that the novel reflects colonial attitudes.

82
00:06:00.629 --> 00:06:04.515
A, is argued. B, argues. C, is arguing.

83
00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:10.055
The answer is A: is argued.

84
00:06:11.217 --> 00:06:14.354
The way texts echo and respond to other texts is blank.

85
00:06:14.654 --> 00:06:18.482
A, subtext. B, intertextuality. C, canon.

86
00:06:22.033 --> 00:06:24.822
The answer is B: intertextuality.

87
00:06:25.983 --> 00:06:28.645
The theory and method of interpretation is blank.

88
00:06:28.945 --> 00:06:33.504
A, deconstruction. B, postmodernism. C, hermeneutics.

89
00:06:37.067 --> 00:06:39.403
The answer is C: hermeneutics.

90
00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:42.306
Choose the correct sentence.

91
00:06:42.606 --> 00:06:49.069
A, It is believed the poem to be ancient.. B, The poem is believed to be ancient.. C, The poem believes to be ancient..

92
00:06:52.633 --> 00:06:56.049
The answer is B: The poem is believed to be ancient..

93
00:06:57.183 --> 00:07:00.448
According to the reading, critics question the canon because…

94
00:07:00.748 --> 00:07:06.910
A, it is too short. B, it may reflect the values of the powerful. C, it ignores close reading.

95
00:07:10.467 --> 00:07:13.743
The answer is B: it may reflect the values of the powerful.

96
00:07:14.933 --> 00:07:18.131
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:18.983 --> 00:07:21.865
How does a critical lens change the meaning of a text?

98
00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:40.139
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:40.439 --> 00:08:16.292
A critical lens changes which features of a text become visible and significant. Read as a romance, Tum Teav celebrates passionate love; read through a feminist lens, it exposes how women's choices were controlled by family and power. It has been argued that no reading is entirely neutral, because every reader brings assumptions. However, a lens does not create meaning from nothing; it foregrounds patterns that are already present. In my view, using several lenses produces the richest understanding.

100
00:08:16.592 --> 00:08:18.394
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:19.283 --> 00:08:23.654
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and passive nominal clauses.

102
00:08:23.954 --> 00:08:30.066
Today's value is intellectual curiosity. Ask deep questions, and keep exploring ideas.

103
00:08:30.366 --> 00:08:32.984
See you in Unit three. Goodbye!

104
00:08:33.284 --> 00:08:36.516
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

